\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A negative even gauge-invariant reflection square}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The \vocabk{cellular paired functional}{fdip-00XQ}
is not reflection positive, even on the gauge-invariant
even part of its strict positive half. For
#{b_1=\bar\psi_{v_1}\psi_{v_1}}, the element
##{f=115+527b_1}
belongs to that part and satisfies
##{\ell((\Theta f)f)=-50.}}

\proof{
\p{Local gauge transformation multiplies both generators
at #{v_1} by the same sign, so #{b_1} is gauge invariant.
It is even, and its support is the strict positive vertex.
Order reversal in the declared reflection gives
#{\Theta b_1=b_0}. By \ref{fdip-00XW} and the partition
sum in \ref{fdip-00XX},
##{\ell(b_0)=\ell(b_1)=-\frac{115}{527},\qquad
   \ell(b_0b_1)=\frac{25}{527}.}
Thus the reflection Gram matrix on #{1,b_1} is
##{\begin{pmatrix}
 1&-115/527\\-115/527&25/527
 \end{pmatrix},\qquad
 \det=\frac{-50}{527^2}.}
More directly, the stated vector has quadratic value
##{115^2-2\cdot115^2+25\cdot527=-50.}
This is negative in both declared ground fields. The
compatibility already proved in \ref{fdip-00XT} makes it a
negative value of a Hermitian reflection form.}
}

\p{The same element disproves positivity on any larger
positive half containing this vertex algebra and retaining
the same reflection and functional.}
